# Environment variables — copy this file to `.env` and fill in the real values.
#
# Versioned on purpose (D-26): it documents which variables exist without
# leaking a single real value. `.env` itself is ignored by `.gitignore`.
#
# HOW IT IS USED — read this before creating a `.env`:
# this project does not load `.env` automatically, because nothing needs it to.
# `config.py` reads `os.environ` directly, so the values have to be set in the
# shell that launches the app. There is no python-dotenv in `requirements.txt`
# on purpose: a package that exists only to read a file the user can export in
# one line is exactly what AGENTS.md forbids.
#
# PowerShell (Windows), current session only:
#   $env:SECRET_KEY = "a long random value"
#   $env:DATABASE_PATH = "instance/pfo2.db"   # optional, this is the default
#   python servidor.py
#
# A `.env` file is still useful as a note to yourself: keep the real values in
# it, copy them into the shell when you work, and never commit it.

# Signing key for the JWT tokens issued by /login (RNF-03, D-03).
# Losing it invalidates every token in circulation; leaking it lets anyone
# forge a valid one. Generate it, do not type a word:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-a-generated-value

# SQLite file path (D-13). Optional: defaults to instance/pfo2.db, which is
# inside Flask's conventional instance directory and ignored by `*.db`.
# The tests never read this value: they use an in-memory database (RNF-12).
# DATABASE_PATH=instance/pfo2.db
